---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Comparisons",
    tags: [
      "premier protein vs ensure",
      "ensure vs premier protein",
      "ensure max protein vs premier protein",
      "premier protein vs ensure for seniors",
      "ensure plus vs premier protein"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Protein Drink Picks",
    link: "/best-protein-drink/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Head-to-Head"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Premier Protein vs Ensure</Fragment>
      One is a 30 g protein shake built for gyms and diets; the other is a family of nutrition drinks built for older adults and people who need calories. Here is which one fits you, by label and by price.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Shakes</a>
      <a href="#which-ensure">Which Ensure?</a>
      <a href="#protein">Protein</a>
      <a href="#calories-sugar">Calories and Sugar</a>
      <a href="#sweeteners">Sweeteners and Ingredients</a>
      <a href="#vitamins">Vitamins and Minerals</a>
      <a href="#price">Price per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#older-adults">Older Adults and Weight Gain</a>
      <a href="#weight-loss">Weight Loss and the Gym</a>
      <a href="#health">Health Caveats</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Premier Protein and Ensure Max Protein are nearly the same drink: 30 g of protein, 1 g of sugar and 150 to 160 calories per 11 oz shake, at about $1.86 and $1.94 per 25 g of protein on Amazon. The answer changes if you mean Ensure Original or Ensure Plus, which trade protein for calories and sugar. This page compares every pairing, the price and who each suits.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Premier Protein vs Ensure: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Premier Protein and Ensure Max Protein are close to identical: both give 30 g of protein and 1 g of sugar in an 11 oz shake, both use milk protein concentrate and calcium caseinate, and both are sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium. Premier was about 8 cents cheaper per 25 g of protein at the Amazon prices we saw. Ensure Plus and Ensure Original are a different kind of drink, with far more calories and sugar and much less protein.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per shake (label)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Premier Protein 30g</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ensure Max Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ensure Plus</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ensure Original</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Size</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">11 oz</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">11 fl oz</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8 fl oz</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8 fl oz</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">16 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">9 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">160</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">150</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">350</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">220</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Total sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 g (0 g added)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 g (0 g added)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">19 g (dark chocolate)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10 g (vanilla)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweetener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, ace-K</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, ace-K</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sugar, maltodextrin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sugar, sucralose</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vitamins and minerals</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 (brand claim)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 (Abbott)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">27 (Abbott)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">27 (Abbott)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Price per shake</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.23</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.33</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.04</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.48</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost per 25 g protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.86</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.94</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $3.18</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $4.12</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            <strong>Pick Premier Protein if</strong> you want the most protein for the fewest calories at the lowest price, for weight loss, after the gym or as a breakfast add-on. <strong>Pick Ensure Max Protein if</strong> you want the same 30 g with a little more fiber and vitamin D, or simply like its flavors. <strong>Pick Ensure Plus if</strong> you, or a parent, need to gain or hold weight. <strong>Pick Ensure Original</strong> only as a light nutrition top-up; it is not a protein shake.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Shakes We Compared, With Amazon Prices
          </h2>

          <p>
            One Premier Protein pack and three Ensure packs, so every pairing on this page has a card. Prices are the Amazon prices we saw on November 16, 2026; they move often, so check the listing before you buy.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="which-ensure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Which Ensure Shake Should You Compare to Premier Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ensure Max Protein is the Ensure shake to compare with Premier Protein, because it is the only mainstream Ensure line built on the same 30 g, low-sugar formula. Most head-to-head articles get this wrong by putting Premier next to Ensure Original, which is like comparing a protein bar with a granola bar. They share a shelf, not a purpose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ensure is a family of drinks made by Abbott, and the lines differ more from each other than Premier differs from Max Protein. Ensure Original gives 220 calories and 9 g of protein in 8 fl oz, per Abbott. Ensure Plus gives 350 calories and 16 g of protein in the same 8 fl oz. Ensure High Protein, per its current Amazon listing, gives 20 g of protein with 26 vitamins and minerals. Ensure Max Protein gives 30 g of protein and 150 calories in 11 fl oz. Abbott also sells a 42 g Max Protein shake and a Max Protein 2-in-1 version with HMB, which this page does not cover.
          </p>

          <p>
            Premier Protein's range is narrower. The classic 30 g shake is the product nearly everyone means, and the brand's site lists it at 160 calories with no added sugar. Premier also sells an Ultimate 42 g shake, a 20 g almondmilk shake, 22 g minis, Coffeehouse and Indulgence flavors, and a whey powder. If you want the powder side of the brand, our <a href="/premier-protein-powder-review/">Premier Protein powder review</a> covers the 30 g, two-scoop tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the real question behind "Premier Protein vs Ensure" is usually one of two things. A lifter or dieter wants to know whether Max Protein is better than Premier, and the answer is that they are nearly the same drink. A family member shopping for an older parent wants to know whether Premier can replace Ensure, and the answer depends on whether that parent needs protein or calories. The rest of this page answers both.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Match the Ensure line before you compare.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Premier 30 g vs Ensure Max Protein is a protein shake against a protein shake. Premier vs Ensure Plus or Original is a protein shake against a calorie and nutrition drink. Decide which question you are asking first.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Has More Protein: Premier Protein or Ensure?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Premier Protein and Ensure Max Protein both have 30 g of protein per shake, per their labels at the time of writing, so neither wins on protein. Ensure Plus has 16 g, Ensure High Protein has 20 g, and Ensure Original has 9 g. Against any Ensure line except Max Protein and the 42 g shake, Premier has more protein per bottle.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein itself is the same in both 30 g shakes. Premier's ingredient list names milk protein concentrate and calcium caseinate as its protein sources, and Abbott's label for Ensure Max Protein names the same two. Both are dairy proteins that are mostly casein, which digests more slowly than whey. That makes both shakes reasonably filling for their calories, and it also means neither is a fast-digesting post-workout whey, if that distinction matters to you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein density is where the shakes split into two groups. In Premier's 30 g shake, 120 of its 160 calories come from protein, about 75%. In Ensure Max Protein, 120 of 150 calories come from protein, about 80%. In Ensure Plus it is 64 of 350 calories, about 18%, and in Ensure Original about 16%. The first two are protein supplements; the second two are mixed-nutrition drinks where carbohydrate and fat do most of the work.
          </p>

          <p>
            How much protein you need decides whether 9 g or 30 g is the right amount. The US RDA is 0.8 g per kilogram of body weight per day, and an expert group convened by the European Union Geriatric Medicine Society (the PROT-AGE study group, 2013) suggested 1.0 to 1.2 g per kilogram for healthy adults over 65. For a 70 kg (154 lb) adult, that is 56 to 84 g a day, so one 30 g shake covers a third to a half of it. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein calculator guide</a> runs the numbers by weight and goal.
          </p>

          <p>
            If protein per bottle is your only criterion, Premier and Ensure Max Protein tie, and both beat the classic Ensure drinks by a wide margin. If you want even more, both brands now sell a 42 g shake, which is a separate comparison.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="calories-sugar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Has Less Sugar and Fewer Calories?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ensure Max Protein has the fewest calories at 150, and Premier Protein has 160; both list 1 g of total sugar and 0 g of added sugar, per their labels. Ensure Plus has 350 calories and 19 g of sugar in the dark chocolate flavor, and Ensure Original has 220 calories and 10 g of sugar in vanilla, per Abbott's store listings.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 10-calorie gap between the two 30 g shakes is not worth choosing on. It comes mostly from fat (3 g in Premier's chocolate shake against 2 g in Max Protein) and from Max Protein's extra fiber, which counts for fewer calories than other carbohydrate. Total carbohydrate is 4 g in Premier's chocolate shake and 6 g in Max Protein, with 2 g and 4 g of fiber respectively. Either one fits a low-carb or calorie-counted diet easily.
          </p>

          <p>
            The classic Ensure drinks are a different story. Per Abbott's label, the dark chocolate Ensure Plus has 47 g of carbohydrate, 18 g of it added sugar, plus 11 g of fat. Ensure Original vanilla has 32 g of carbohydrate with 9 g of added sugar. Corn maltodextrin is the second ingredient in both, after water, so most of the carbohydrate comes from a fast-digesting starch and sugar rather than fiber.
          </p>

          <p>
            That sugar is a feature for some readers and a problem for others. For someone who struggles to eat enough, 350 easy calories in 8 fl oz is exactly the point of Ensure Plus. For someone trying to lose weight or keep blood sugar steady, one bottle carries more added sugar than many people aim for in a whole meal. Abbott itself sells Glucerna, a separate line, for people with diabetes, which tells you Ensure Plus is not designed for that job.
          </p>

