        <div id="sweeteners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Do Premier Protein and Ensure Use Artificial Sweeteners?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes: Premier Protein and Ensure Max Protein are both sweetened with sucralose and acesulfame potassium, per their ingredient lists at the time of writing. Ensure Original uses sugar plus sucralose. The Ensure Plus dark chocolate ingredient list we checked names sugar and corn maltodextrin and no artificial sweetener, though it does list natural and artificial flavors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Side by side, the two 30 g ingredient lists read almost like the same recipe. Premier's chocolate shake lists filtered water, milk protein concentrate, calcium caseinate, cocoa, a little sunflower or soybean oil, flavors, cellulose gel and gum, salt, sucralose, acesulfame potassium, carrageenan, phosphates and a vitamin and mineral blend. Ensure Max Protein uses the same two proteins, the same two sweeteners and a similar list of thickeners and minerals. If one upsets your stomach, the other probably will too.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both 30 g shakes contain carrageenan, a seaweed-derived thickener that some buyers choose to avoid. Premier's label states that it contains milk and soy. Abbott's Ensure labels list milk, and the Original and Plus lists include soy protein isolate and soy lecithin. Abbott describes Ensure Max Protein and Original as gluten-free and suitable for lactose intolerance (not for galactosemia), and Premier's Amazon listing calls its shake gluten-free.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want to avoid sucralose entirely, neither 30 g shake works. The practical options are an unsweetened or stevia-sweetened powder (see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">protein powders without artificial sweeteners</a>) or, if calories are the goal, Ensure Plus, which uses sugar instead. Taste follows the sweetener: buyers commonly describe both 30 g shakes as sweet with a slight aftertaste, and flavor preference is the most common reason people pick one over the other. Our <a href="/best-premier-protein-flavor/">best Premier Protein flavor guide</a> sums up what buyers say about each Premier flavor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vitamins" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Has More Vitamins and Minerals?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ensure has more: Abbott lists 25 vitamins and minerals in Ensure Max Protein and 27 in Ensure Original and Ensure Plus, while Premier Protein claims 24. The bigger difference is the amount of a few key nutrients, not the count, and vitamin D is the clearest example.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ensure Max Protein lists 11 mcg of vitamin D (60% of the Daily Value) per shake, per Abbott's strawberry label. Premier's chocolate shake lists 6 mcg (30% DV). Abbott says Ensure Original now has 8 mcg. Both 30 g shakes list 650 mg of calcium, 50% of the Daily Value. For older adults, who often fall short on vitamin D and calcium, that makes Max Protein the slightly stronger supplement if the rest of the diet is thin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Premier's panel is broad for a protein shake. Its chocolate label lists vitamins A, C, D, E and K, eight B vitamins at 20% of the Daily Value each, and minerals including phosphorus, magnesium, zinc, selenium, copper and iron. The brand markets the vitamin C and E content for immune support. Potassium is low in both 30 g shakes, at 320 mg in Premier and 390 mg in Max Protein, which is relevant for people on potassium-restricted diets (see the health section below).
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this makes either shake a multivitamin replacement on its own, and doubling up has a cost. Someone drinking two or three fortified shakes a day plus a daily multivitamin can stack vitamin A, vitamin D and minerals well above what they need. If you or a parent already takes supplements, check the totals with a pharmacist, especially for vitamin D and vitamin K (which matters for people on warfarin).
          </p>
        </div>

