        <div id="older-adults" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Is Premier Protein or Ensure Better for Older Adults?
          </h2>

          <p>
            It depends on whether the older adult needs more protein or more calories. For a parent who eats normal meals but falls short on protein, Premier Protein or Ensure Max Protein adds 30 g for about 150 to 160 calories. For a parent who is losing weight, has a small appetite or is recovering from illness, Ensure Plus at 350 calories per 8 fl oz is usually the better tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ensure's brand was built around this use, and Abbott markets Ensure Plus as clinically shown to help adults gain weight, per its Amazon listing. The 8 fl oz bottle matters as much as the calories: many older adults find an 11 oz protein shake hard to finish, and a shake left half drunk delivers half the nutrition. If finishing is a problem, the smaller Ensure bottle, or splitting a 30 g shake across the morning, can beat a larger bottle on paper.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unintended weight loss and muscle loss (sarcopenia) are real concerns with age, and protein helps with the second. The PROT-AGE group's suggestion of 1.0 to 1.2 g of protein per kilogram a day for healthy older adults, and more for those with acute or chronic illness, is higher than many older adults reach. A 30 g shake closes much of that gap. Ensure Max Protein's extra vitamin D gives it a small edge over Premier here, while Premier costs slightly less.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is no reason an older adult cannot use both: an Ensure Plus between meals for calories and a 30 g shake with a light breakfast for protein. The caveats are sugar, kidney function and medications, which the health section below covers. Anyone with a chronic condition or unexplained weight loss should have a doctor or registered dietitian choose the product and the amount. For powders aimed at this group, see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-seniors/">best protein powder for seniors</a>, and for calorie-dense options our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-gain/">weight gain guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-loss" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Is Premier Protein or Ensure Better for Weight Loss and the Gym?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Premier Protein and Ensure Max Protein are both good for weight loss, and Premier is slightly cheaper; Ensure Plus and Ensure Original are poor choices for weight loss because of their calories and sugar. A 30 g, 150-to-160-calorie shake with 1 g of sugar is about as efficient as a ready-to-drink protein gets.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein helps a diet in two ways that matter here: it is the most filling macronutrient per calorie, and enough of it helps preserve muscle while you eat less. Swapping a 220-calorie Ensure Original for a 160-calorie Premier saves 60 calories and adds 21 g of protein. Swapping an Ensure Plus for Premier saves 190 calories and 18 g of sugar. Those are the trades that make Premier the more popular diet shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            For lifters, both 30 g shakes deliver a full serving of protein in a bottle that needs no shaker or fridge until opened. Because the protein is mostly casein, it digests more slowly than a whey isolate, which matters less than daily protein total for most people. If you want a faster whey shake or more choice, our <a href="/best-protein-drink/">best protein drinks</a> roundup compares the ready-to-drink field, and our <a href="/muscle-milk-vs-premier-protein/">Muscle Milk vs Premier Protein</a> comparison covers the other gym-shelf rival.
          </p>

          <p>
            One warning for dieters: neither 30 g shake is a meal. At 150 to 160 calories with little carbohydrate and fat, drinking one in place of lunch is a large calorie cut that most people cannot sustain. Pair the shake with fruit, a sandwich or oats, or look at a purpose-built option in our <a href="/best-meal-replacement-shake/">best meal replacement shake</a> guide. For powders built around a calorie deficit, see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-loss/">weight loss protein powder</a> picks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="health" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Who Should Check With a Doctor Before Drinking Either?
          </h2>

          <p>
            People with kidney disease, diabetes, a condition that causes unintended weight loss, or a diet set by their care team should ask a doctor or registered dietitian before using Premier Protein or Ensure regularly. Both are foods, not medicines, but they are concentrated enough that the wrong one can work against a treatment plan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kidney disease is the clearest case. People with chronic kidney disease who are not on dialysis are often told to limit protein, and a 30 g shake can be a large share of a restricted daily allowance. Both 30 g shakes also carry meaningful phosphorus from added phosphates. People on dialysis have different needs again, and Abbott sells a separate line, Nepro, for them. Neither Premier nor standard Ensure is a renal formula.
          </p>

          <p>
            For diabetes, the 30 g shakes are the gentler choice on blood sugar, with 4 to 6 g of carbohydrate and 1 g of sugar per label. Ensure Plus, with 47 g of carbohydrate and 19 g of sugar, and Ensure Original, with 32 g of carbohydrate, can raise blood sugar quickly. Abbott's Glucerna line is designed for people with diabetes, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-diabetics/">protein powder for diabetics</a> guide covers powders.
          </p>

          <p>
            Medications and allergies come next. Both brands contain milk, and Premier's label adds soy. Fortified vitamin K can interfere with warfarin if intake swings day to day. Anyone using shakes as their main food source, after surgery or during an illness, should have a clinician choose the product, because a complete meal replacement needs more calories, fat and carbohydrate than a 30 g shake provides. This page is a label comparison, not medical advice; see <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> for the general picture.
          </p>
        </div>

